1 NOTE {geni:about_me} === GEDCOM Note ===
2 CONT Øyster Vang no. 744 was at 1 sp. 1 øre 4 mark. tax — after land
2 CONT registration divided among these owners:
2 CONT •	Reinskloster (later called Bakke Monastery) ………… “1–16
2 CONT •	Leksvik Church ………………………………………… “1–”
2 CONT •	Ytterøy Church ………………………………………… “1–”
2 CONT •	Vestvik Church ………………………………………… “1–”
2 CONT
2 CONT Tenant in 1668 was Lars Jonson. On the farm they could keep 6
2 CONT cattle and 1 horse, and sow 5 tønner grain. From sometime in the 1790s onward
2 CONT until 1750 Øyster Vang 2 was without a permanent tenant, the users
2 CONT on agreement for the taxes or a certain fee, were called “ødegård” (deserted farm), as it
2 CONT was termed.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1762 Anders Olson Vang bought both Øyster Vang farms
2 CONT at auction for 400 rdl. In 1770 Jacob Matheson Schultz
2 CONT deeded the same properties from the 3rd to the 27th of January, when Anders Olson
2 CONT again received deed for 880 rdl. A while later Anders
2 CONT Olson exchanged the Øyster Vang farms with the Vester Vang that Major Anders
2 CONT Bull owned. The farm price is in the exchange deed recorded at 800 rdl.
2 CONT From then on, the farm parts of Øyster Vang came under the same owner
2 CONT and were used as one; they are also in the tax records listed as one,
2 CONT but with 2 matrikel numbers. In 1780 Bull sold the farm to Jørgen Tore-
2 CONT son Grannan for 800 rdl.
